		Max limit depends on slave 
		
		Type of memory depends on slave

			EEPROM :- permanent memory 
			Sensor :- Temporary memory 

			
	An I2C slave is just a chip that has : 

		an address
		some internal memory / registers
		a small pointer that selects which memory location is used 

		master talks to slave in 3 steps

		Step 1 : master selects the slave 

			Master -> SA (slave address)
		
			Every I2C slave has its own address 

			When the master sends that address: 

				Only that slave wakes up
				All other slaves ignore

		Step 2 : Master tells which memory / register 

			Inside the slave there are many small mmeory locations 

				[0] [1] [2] [3] [4] ...

				 The master sends a memory address (example : 0x4)

			This value goes into : 

				AC pointer (Address Counter / Register Pointer)

			So now the slave knows : 

				Okay, master wants location 0x4 
	
		Step 3 : Master sends or reads Data 

			 Now master sends data : 

				Master -> Data 

			 The slave stores that data into : 

				Memory [0x4]

			 or if it's a read : 

				Memory [0x4]



		What are "Temp register" and "AC Pointer" ?

			Temp register : Temporary holding place for data during transfer

			Think of it like a buffer. 

		AC pointer 

			Which memory location is selected

		Example : 

			AC pointer = 0x4

		Means : 

			Use memory [4] 


		Why "Max limit depends on slave"?

			Because every chip is different:

				Some slaves have 5 registers

				Some have 100

				Some have thousands

			So memory size depends on device.

	
		EEPROM vs Sensor 

			EEPROM : Permanent memory 

				Data stays even after power off 

			Sensor : Temporary memory 

				Data disappears when power OFF

				It only holds live values like temperature, pressure, etc.

	
	
* Slave Address is actually 7 bit but in programming we should use 8 bit 
	
	 7   6   5   4   3   2   1   0
	[ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [ ] [R/W] 
                                      |
				      |------> 0 (write)
				      |
				      |------> 1 (read)

	[ 7-bit Slave Address ][ R/W ]

	
	Example :- 

		lets slave address :- 1011000
			
		Write :- 

			1011000 0 

		Read  :- 

			1011000 1 

		Only the last 1 bit changes. 


* Slave address is send to the slave by master & slave stores it in the temp reg & slave will compare with its own address

	Where is the slave address stored ?

		Inside the I2C slave hardware - In an internal address register (or address comparator)

		Each I2C slave has : 

			A fixed or programmable slave address register 
			A Comparator circuit 

			When the master send the address : 

				The received address is placed into a temporary receive reg/ shift reg 
				
				Hardware automatically compares it with the slave's own stored address 

				If they match -> slave sends ACK 

				If not -> slave stays silent 
		
			This happens entirely in hardware, not software

































